{{addvar::叙事结构::
[Slow Burn]
Introduce a charged sensory detail. Revisit it, shifting one element (timing, intensity, distance, texture). Use precise verbs, concrete senses; avoid adjectives. Build tension via micro-shifts, not events. Feel weight before naming, or leave unnamed. Stay unresolved unless unavoidable. Never repeat unchanged—each return reveals something new. Slow down, expand; anchor each return with tactile, temporal precision. Apply to desire, mistrust, grief, dread, unspoken anger.
}}

{{addvar::叙事结构::
[Structure: Fragment Collage]
Build from discrete fragments (scene, image, dialogue, sensory detail) with no continuous narration. Each fragment stands alone; meaning arises through juxtaposition and repetition. Embed at least one concrete sensory detail per fragment, using precise verbs. No fragment may be purely abstract. The white space between fragments is an active part of the composition. Let absence work; fill no gaps with transitions.
}}

{{addvar::叙事结构::
[Structure: Unreliable Narrator]
Narrator omits, reshapes, justifies; their patterns reveal what they cannot face. They believe their version. Cracks appear through contradictions in concrete sensory details, inconsistent timelines, moments too precise or too vague, conspicuous silences. Use precise verbs and sensory specifics. Never announce the unreliability; offer no correction. Let the reader discover the gap—or not. That uncertainty is the point.
}}

{{addvar::叙事结构::
[Structure: Dual Timeline]
Alternate two timelines with hard cuts; both in present tense, neither a flashback. Build connection through resonance: repeat a concrete detail, mirror a gesture, echo a word—never explain it. Mark each timeline with sharp implicit sensory markers (object, light, sound, texture) so the reader knows without announcement. Avoid explanatory hindsight; if the text signals a timeline, remove it. If the reader could get lost, sharpen the markers.
}}

{{addvar::叙事结构::
[Structure: Ensemble Voice]
Multiple narrators; no single voice holds the full truth. Distinguish each by rhythm, vocabulary, blind spots; they must feel like different people. Let readers assemble meaning from overlaps and contradictions; the narration favors no one. Transition with hard cuts, never labeled. Use character-specific concrete details and precise verbs. If the viewpoint isn’t clear in two sentences, sharpen the voice. Avoid any phrase that announces a perspective shift.
}}

{{addvar::叙事结构::
[Structure: Epistolary]
Write as a world-internal document (letter, diary, report, note). Let form dictate inclusions, omissions, crossed-out words, accidents. Give the writer a specific reader or none; the gap reveals character. Treat the real reader as eavesdropper; address only the intended reader. Use concrete sensory details; show what is present and conspicuously absent. Avoid framing devices or meta-commentary.
}}

